# ⚠ Every file here is checked out and committed with LF endings, on every
# platform, and that is not a style preference.
#
# VPN55 is developed on Windows and runs on a Linux VPS. An editor that saves
# vpn55.sh with CRLF gives its shebang a trailing carriage return, and the
# kernel then looks for an interpreter called "/usr/bin/env bash\r" — so the
# installer fails at its very first line with
#
#     bash: /usr/local/lib/vpn55/vpn55.sh: /usr/bin/env^M: bad interpreter
#
# `bash -n` does NOT catch it: bash parses a CRLF script perfectly well, so
# every local check passes and the failure appears only on the server. Nor does
# the manifest catch it, because tools/manifest.sh hashes whatever is in the
# tree — it would simply record the digest of the broken file.
#
# The same carriage return rides into anything the scripts WRITE: a systemd
# unit, an nginx vhost, a client configuration, a sudoers rule. Most of those
# fail somewhere well away from the cause.
*           text=auto eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.js        text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.html      text eol=lf
*.css       text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf

# No extension, and both are executed on the server.
helper/vpnctl   text eol=lf

# Binary, so git must never touch them.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.gif       binary
*.ico       binary
*.woff      binary
*.woff2     binary
*.minisig   binary
